    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "The main idea in this line of work is to ﬁnd P and Y (x) such that"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 42,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 345.961, 280.829, 545.786, 292.63 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "−Θ(x) − Θ⊤(x) > 0 ∀x ∈ Rn \\ {0}, (12)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 43,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 306.72, 192.75, 545.836, 268.472 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "for all systems (A,B) compatible with the data. In the earlier work [8], H(x) is taken to be equal to the identity matrix, which implies that Z(x) = F(x). In contrast, [9] considers more general Z(x) satisfying (10). This strategy is appealing because it leads to data-based linear matrix inequalities for control design. Unfortunately, however, the method also has some major limitations."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 44,
    "level" : "2",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.96, 159.629, 545.759, 181.98 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "(1) The matrix ∂Z∂x (x) must have full row rank for all x ∈ Rn \\ {0}."
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 45,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 382.308, 175.308, 392.988, 175.788 ],
